  "tldr": "Worst-case propensity-ratio caps can be driven by rare histories, while a second-moment budget measures average confounding strength. For the fixed two-decision regime \\((1,1)\\), the compatible mean set is characterized as an exact compact interval generated by a convex perspective program. A conditional projection defines the observed-law ratio \\(h_0^{\\mathrm{pr}}\\) separately from the original full-law ratio \\(h_0\\), restores the required early-history factorization, and supports compatible laws attaining the endpoints; a two-global-price conditional-root dual handles active, zero-price, and unit-budget faces. The statistical frontier is an explicit cross-fitted optimized-value score with two indispensable propensity-floor corrections; proving its uniform quadratic bias and nested-root stability yields a joint multiplier band, whose deterministic range-extended monotone inversion remains honest at tied, flat, or absent breakdown crossings.",
  "project_justification": {
    "gap": "Zhang and Zhao's Proposition 3, Theorem 1, and Section 4.4 treat a one-treatment second-moment model, whereas Tan treats sequential compatibility under range-bounded ratios; neither covers the two-decision transported-perspective model and its coupled inference problem.",
    "niche": "Sequential normalization couples an early ratio to the late ratio through a product weight and a transported perspective moment, producing a two-price optimizer and a score whose clipping floors both move with the observed law.",
    "fill": "The project derives exact compatible sharpness, attainment, boundary-correct duality, and a falsifiable route to uniform endpoint and breakdown-region inference under primitive learner and clipping conditions."
  },
  "related_work": "Tan's Lemma 1 supplies the full-law compatibility and constructive pasting logic, but his sensitivity ratios are bounded pointwise. Zhang and Zhao's Proposition 3 supplies the clipped-affine sensitivity-value optimizer, Theorem 1 supplies the average-case influence-function template, and Section 4.4 supplies the multiplier-band architecture, all for one treatment. Their one-treatment marginal second-moment model does not contain the two-decision sequential normalization or transported perspective moment used here. Bonvini, Kennedy, Ventura, and Wasserman supply a longitudinal influence-function recursion for different sensitivity restrictions and therefore inform rather than subsume the score. Dorn, Guo, and Kallus connect sharp sensitivity optimization to efficient endpoint estimation in a one-treatment odds-ratio model. Chernozhukov et al. supply cross-fitting and orthogonal-score remainder control. Ren, Telschow, and Schwartzman supply deterministic inverse-set coverage; Masten and Poirier motivate breakdown frontiers; Fang and Santos explain why ordinary smooth bootstrap arguments cannot be imposed at ties.",
  "technical_internal_limitation": "The general conditional-root stability and uniform quadratic held-out bias bound are not treated as established. The Gaussian endpoint band and the resulting full-ray confidence claim are conditional on a positive resolution of Open-ended Question 1. The early kill test perturbs every component of a finite two-baseline, two-history, three-outcome law with active clipping at both stages and compares constrained-value derivatives with score covariances, then jointly perturbs nuisances and prices. A reproducible first-order mismatch kills the inference theorem. Gaussian shrinking bands are asserted only on \\(S\\); zero-price and unit-budget faces receive deterministic outcome-range coverage, and learned dynamic regimes are outside scope.",
  "honest_scope": "Population sharpness, the two-price dual, compatible attainment, and the deterministic simultaneous-band inversion lemma are theorem obligations. The main open content is the general nonparametric proof of conditional-root stability, score continuity, and the quadratic remainder; the Gaussian endpoint process and full-ray statistical coverage depend on resolving it. At \\((\\Sigma_0,\\Sigma_1)=(1,1)\\), the interval reduces to the observed target-path mean. No inference is claimed for a learned policy, and no shrinking Gaussian approximation is claimed on excluded faces.",
